The issue was that `np.dtypes.StringDType` was being converted to fixed-length UTF32 (`dtype('<U3')`) when writing to Zarr in `xarray`, which triggered a `UnstableSpecificationWarning` and prevented proper roundtripping.

The root cause was in `xarray/backends/zarr.py` within the `encode_zarr_variable` function, where `EncodedStringCoder` and `ensure_fixed_length_bytes` were applied to all string-like variables. By bypassing these for variables with `dtype.kind == 'T'` (the kind for `StringDType`), Zarr V3 can handle these types natively.

I implemented a check in `xarray/backends/zarr.py` to skip the manual fixed-length encoding for `StringDType` variables:

```python
    if var.dtype.kind != "T":
        coder = coding.strings.EncodedStringCoder(allows_unicode=True)
        var = coder.encode(var, name=name)
        var = coding.strings.ensure_fixed_length_bytes(var)
```

This allows `StringDType` to be preserved during the Zarr write/read cycle.
